WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.240
And so what we found was if you had

2
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.860
a good network connection for a little bit

3
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.460
and you caught some telemetry

4
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.340
and try to push it up

5
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.230
to a big database in the cloud, it worked.

6
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.650
But that really isn't the normal case.

7
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.040
A lot of time your network isn't good

8
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.320
or you walk from inside to outside

9
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.120
or into a tunnel

10
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.110
and then suddenly your network connection drops.

11
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.360
We were seeing

12
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.330
that the data was being dropped on the ground.

13
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.190
That still is the case.

14
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.360
We'll try it for a few times

15
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.520
and then drop it on the floor.

16
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.140
When you have those really frustrating experiences

17
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.360
where you don't have enough bars

18
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.460
and if you do have bars,

19
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.220
it's not working

20
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.920
and you can't get to your car

21
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.420
or couldn't see that it was there

22
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.610
or it wasn't updating,

23
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.590
that was when we lost the visibility.

24
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:35.700
And so when your customers

25
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.740
are having a bad experience,

26
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:37.800
they'll go to your big competitor

27
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.350
and maybe never come back.

28
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.050
So it's existential to us to have that visibility.

29
00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.860
So the first version really was collecting all

30
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.180
that data onto the phone itself

31
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.660
and just making sure

32
00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.260
that we could upload it later

33
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.280
when we did have a good network connection.

34
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.670
Sounds very simple.

35
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.130
It's actually really hard to do.

36
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:55.640
You don't want to blow

37
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.680
out the storage on the device

38
00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:58.680
so you have to make sure

39
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.270
you're not collecting too much data.

40
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.830
You don't want to affect the user experience.

41
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.100
You have about 16 milliseconds before you start

42
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.710
to a human can notice a lag or freeze.

43
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:08.880
And so how do you do

44
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:09.880
and collect a lot of data

45
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.180
without affecting the rest

46
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.430
of your app's performance?

47
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.020
So a lot of the initial work

48
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.180
we did was around performance

49
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.250
and memory safety,

50
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.100
making sure that everything was asynchronous

51
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.410
and didn't affect the UX.

52
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.000
We had a lot of work on reliability,

53
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.270
detecting when it was working well or not.

54
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.080
So we ended up with a solution

55
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.270
that really wrapped a sort of native library.

56
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.200
We built it in Rust

57
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.190
and it still is to this day.

58
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.970
It gave us a lot of speed, performance,

59
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:34.790
and memory safety.

60
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.120
And so we wrapped that

61
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.830
in sort of native layers,

62
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:38.770
Swift and Kotlin.

63
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.190
So a lot of that work was done there.

64
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.880
And so that really was the use cases

65
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:43.820
that we were trying to do

66
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.300
were the Lyft consumer cases.

67
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.120
In fact, we actually were at the time exploring

68
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.270
writing our own native networking stack based on Envoy.

69
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.740
One of my co-founders had done

70
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.610
the server version of that Envoy proxy,

71
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:56.770
Matt Klein did that.

72
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:58.750
And we were looking at Envoy Mobile.

73
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.260
And so debugging a networking stack

74
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.780
and all of the varied conditions

75
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.160
that the network sees out in the wild,

76
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.480
that was the initial use case

77
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:06.540
that we started this on

78
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.740
and expanded it since then.

79
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.380
The other part that we faced

80
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.040
is where the Lyft

81
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:11.770
was still scaling quite a lot.

82
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.240
And so we had tens of millions of clients

83
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.180
on a daily basis,

84
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.830
like connecting and uploading data.

85
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:20.490
And so at that scale,

86
00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.300
most of their solutions are too expensive

87
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.370
or they couldn't scale to that level.

88
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.440
I know we took down a couple of providers

89
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.080
when we tried to turn them on

90
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.310
past five or six percent.

91
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.340
So I think the other thing,

92
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.390
and they handled it by sampling.

93
00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:33.540
And so the other thing

94
00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.000
that we really wanted to solve

95
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.160
was being able to deal

96
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.330
with the mobile scale,

97
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.250
which is millions and millions of devices.
